\chapter{Network, TLS, QUIC, and HTTP kernels}
\label{ch:103}
\index{Network, TLS, QUIC, and HTTP kernels}

The native network stack includes bounded protocol-specific kernels and a separate documented QUIC/HTTP3 client profile. For the QUIC/HTTP3 recipe, inspect the native UDP and TLS dependencies and the precise supported client operations, including the HTTP/3 GET path. Registered declaration inspection and native C archive plans exist for these kernels. Their resource-bearing client and buffer API remains outside the documented callable On1x profiles.

\section{Test the layers separately}
Use a controlled fixture for transport and a protocol-level oracle for headers, data and shutdown. For a native current client recipe, check the expected target and companion TLS/UDP objects; a build of header-only parsing does not exercise a live client. At the source level, the standard-library HTTP module follows its own runtime and supported interface. Validate transport authorization and peer configuration independently before comparing two implementations. Distinguish application parsing, TLS state, connection state and ownership when debugging failures.

\section{Decode one HTTP/3 envelope in installments}
Use the frame reader with a payload limit of two bytes. In hexadecimal
the sequence \OnxCodeTerm{00 02 41 42} is a DATA frame: a one-byte type,
a one-byte length and the two bytes of its body. Present only the first
three bytes. The length is already known and permitted, but the body
is incomplete. The reader reports MORE and leaves its frame and
consumed-count outputs unchanged. Present all four bytes and expect
success, four bytes consumed, and a borrowed view of the final two.
Keep that backing array alive while examining the view. The frame
reader has not allocated an independent response body for the caller.

Repeat with a payload limit of one and only the first two bytes. This
time the result is LIMIT, not MORE: no later arrival can make the
advertised length fit. For a different negative case use GOAWAY with
a complete one-byte payload whose first two bits specify a two-byte
integer. The outer frame is complete but its one identifier is not.
Waiting for more stream data would read past the end of that frame;
the reader reports invalid and preserves the outputs.

These tests can run on fixed arrays with no peer at all. They establish
where a stream parser should retry and where it should stop. To test
the live client, separately observe peer authentication, the negotiated
application protocol and the outcome of the request. Feeding the above
plaintext to the codec cannot supply any of those observations.

When adapting the reader to a receive buffer, advance the cursor only
by a consumed count from a successful decode. On MORE, keep the
prefix for the next installment; on LIMIT, appending further bytes
does not change the configured maximum.

